<Project>

    <!--
      Prerelease packages must be accepted deliberately, not inherited.

      NuGetAudit already fails the build on a KNOWN VULNERABILITY. Nothing warns about a package that is
      simply not finished. That gap is not theoretical here: `OpenTelemetry.Exporter.Prometheus.AspNetCore`
      was pinned at a beta and stayed pinned, and it took a deliberate check of the NuGet feed on 2026-08-06
      to discover it has been in prerelease for FOUR YEARS — 33 versions, not one stable, since 2022-08-18 —
      while every other package in the same OpenTelemetry suite shipped stable 1.17.0 on the same day.

      A pin nobody re-examines becomes a decision nobody made. So: every prerelease version in
      Directory.Packages.props raises OVERFITPRERELEASE at build time, unless it is listed below with a
      reason and a date. Accepting one is then an explicit, reviewable act rather than silence.

      Warning, never error, and on purpose. A prerelease dependency is a judgement call about support and
      risk, not a rule violation — promoting it to an error would only teach people to add entries to the
      accept-list without reading them.

      Emitted once, from Main, rather than once per project: twenty-five copies of the same warning is how a
      warning becomes wallpaper.

      Turn it off for one build with -p:OverfitPrereleaseCheck=false. If this file itself misbehaves, deleting
      it restores the previous behaviour exactly — it adds nothing else.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          Accepted prereleases. One entry per package, each with WHY and WHEN, so the next reader can judge
          whether the reason still holds instead of assuming somebody thought about it.

          Nothing is accepted, and as of 2026-08-11 there is nothing to accept: the tree has NO prerelease
          pin at all. `OpenTelemetry.Exporter.Prometheus.AspNetCore` was the one, was deliberately never
          listed here, and is now gone — Demo/LocalAgentAspNetDemo writes its own Prometheus exposition
          (Observability/BucketHistogram.cs + MetricsCollector.WriteExposition), which is what the product
          already does in Sources/Server.AspNet and Sources/Anomalies. XC-6 in docs/TASKS.md carries the
          reasoning, including why hand-rolling beat the stable OTLP exporter here: the demo's compose stack
          SCRAPES /metrics, so OTLP would have needed a collector added to it.

          That is this check working as intended, and the only evidence it has: a pin nobody re-examined for
          four years became visible, then became a decision, then became a removal. Keep the list empty if
          you can — an entry here is a standing exception, not a fix.
        -->
        <!-- <OverfitAcceptedPrerelease Include="Some.Package" Reason="why" Since="yyyy-mm-dd" /> -->
    </ItemGroup>

    <Target Name="OverfitPrereleaseCheck"
            BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
            Condition="'$(OverfitPrereleaseCheck)' != 'false' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Main'">

        <ItemGroup>
            <_OverfitPrerelease Include="@(PackageVersion)"
                                Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(PackageVersion.Version)').Contains('-'))" />
            <_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted Include="@(_OverfitPrerelease)"
                                          Exclude="@(OverfitAcceptedPrerelease)" />
        </ItemGroup>

        <!--
          The Condition is load-bearing, not defensive. Measured 2026-08-07: with the only prerelease
          package added to the accept-list above, `_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted` is correctly empty — and
          the Warning task STILL ran once, batching over the empty list and producing

              " is pinned at a PRERELEASE version () and is not on the accepted list."

          with no package name and no version. That is worse than having no accept-list at all: accepting
          a package deliberately left the build shouting about nothing in particular. An empty item list
          must skip the task, and only an explicit emptiness check makes it do so.
        -->
        <Warning Condition="'@(_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted)' != ''"
                 Code="OVERFITPRERELEASE"
                 File="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Directory.Packages.props"
                 Text="%(_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted.Identity) is pinned at a PRERELEASE version (%(_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted.Version)) and is not on the accepted list. Check whether a stable release exists before keeping it: a package can sit in prerelease for years while the rest of its suite ships stable. Accept it deliberately by adding an OverfitAcceptedPrerelease entry with a reason and a date in Directory.Build.targets, or move off it." />

    </Target>

</Project>
